= April 2022 — The cost of separation

Prime Minister <span class="party-pis"><b>Mateusz Morawiecki</b></span> promises the fastest possible separation
from Russian coal, oil and gas. Utilities search for replacement contracts
while households open higher bills and Silesian trade unionists hear another
transition timetable written without them.

The argument is no longer whether resistance to Russian aggression has a
price. It is who pays that price now, who owns the replacement system, and
whether a temporary return to coal becomes permanent by political convenience.

- @apr22_windfall
- @apr22_public
- @apr22_coal

@apr22_windfall
title: Pair the embargo with a windfall levy and household shield
subtitle: Make profitable energy firms finance immediate protection while leaving ownership unresolved.
on-arrival: household_policy_pressure += 5; climate_policy_pressure += 2; labor_credibility += 4; left_economic_position -= 3; ko_relation -= 1; konf_poll_momentum += 0.1; last_policy_stage = "Proposal"; last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary opposition"; last_policy_implementation = 0; news_headline = "The Left assigns the war-energy bill to excess profits"

Lewica publishes a temporary levy on extraordinary energy profits and uses the
proceeds for a household price shield. Unions and tenants' groups receive an
answer that does not ask them to demonstrate solidarity through arrears.

Private and state energy firms organise against the levy, and KO disputes its
design. We have assigned the immediate bill without yet deciding who will own
the post-Russian energy system.

- @poland_foreign_events.hungary_election_2022: Follow the Hungarian result


@apr22_public
title: Make public energy and rapid transition the replacement
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: No organisational capacity for the programme.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; climate_policy_pressure += 3; administrative_reform_pressure += 4; public_trust += 1; labor_dissent += 2; rural_support -= 1; household_security -= 1; tactical_desertion_to_p2050 += 0.6; last_policy_stage = "Proposal"; last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary opposition"; last_policy_implementation = 0; news_headline = "Public investment becomes the answer to Russian energy"

Municipal utilities, grid engineers and public banks enter one investment plan
for insulation, renewables and a stronger public energy sector. The proposal
turns emergency imports into a timetable for replacing them rather than a new
set of private contracts.

It is expensive and slow beside the next bill. Rural communities and some
industrial unions hear transition before they hear protection, leaving Lewica
to prove that public ownership will reach them in time.

- @poland_foreign_events.hungary_election_2022: Follow the Hungarian result


@apr22_coal
title: Protect coal regions and accept a temporary retreat
subtitle: Secure jobs and dispatchable power now without pretending the climate timetable survives unchanged.
on-arrival: climate_policy_pressure -= 4; industrial_logistics_left_affinity += 2; rural_support += 2; progressive_credibility -= 3; labor_credibility += 2; last_policy_stage = "Proposal"; last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary opposition"; last_policy_implementation = 0; news_headline = "A coal retreat reopens the Left's transition fight"

Mining unions and coal-region mayors receive a guarantee against closures made
in the middle of an energy emergency. The line reassures workers who have heard
too many national plans treat their communities as a footnote.

Temporary extraction acquires contractors, payrolls and a parliamentary
constituency almost immediately. Climate organisers warn that the retreat will
outlive the emergency unless Lewica attaches a dated route back out.

- @poland_foreign_events.hungary_election_2022: Follow the Hungarian result
